es","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Moroccan cuisine","dot":"#e46a3f","intro":[["Babbouche is a dish originating in Morocco whose main ingredient is snails. The snails are cooked slowly in broth that contains ingredients such as thyme, aniseed, gum arabic, mint, caraway and liquorice."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-babbouche"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Babbouche"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Moroccan cuisine","cuisine/moroccan-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":7,"links":[["Anise","ingredient/anise"],["Elettaria cardamomum","ingredient/elettaria-cardamomum"],["Liquorice","ingredient/liquorice"],["Mentha pulegium","ingredient/mentha-pulegium"],["Ras el hanout","ingredient/ras-el-hanout"],["Snail","ingredient/snail"],["Thymus","ingredient/thymus"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"My Moroccan Food","ref":"site/mymoroccanfood-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"mymoroccanfood.com","note":"Moroccan recipe blog linked to cookbook author Nargisse Benkabbou, covering breads, soups and tagines.","url":"https://www.mymoroccanfood.com/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Taste of Maroc","ref":"site/tasteofmaroc-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"tasteofmaroc.com","note":"Moroccan recipes and tutorials from Christine Benlafquih, formerly the Moroccan food writer at The Spruce Eats.","url":"https://tasteofmaroc.com/about/","suggested":true,"kind":"site"},{"name":"Salima's Kitchen","ref":"site/salimaskitchen-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"salimaskitchen.com","note":"Moroccan section of Salima Benkhalti's blog, drawing on her family cooking.","url":"https://salimaskitchen.com/category/moroccan/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Moroccan cuisine","links":[["Cabbage roll","dish/cabbage-roll"],["Couscous","dish/couscous"],["Harira","dish/harira"],["Kourdass","dish/kourdass"],["Medfouna","dish/medfouna"],["Mhancha","dish/mhancha"],["Pastilla","dish/pastilla"],["Restaurant Marrakesh","dish/restaurant-marrakesh"],["Sellou","dish/sellou"],["Tajine lham-lahlou","dish/tajine-lham-lahlou"],["Taktouka","dish/taktouka"],["Tehane","dish/tehane"],["Zaazaa","dish/zaazaa"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/6/62/Snail_dish_in_Marrakesh_1.jpg/500px-Snail_dish_in_Marrakesh_1.jpg","w":500,"h":333,"by":"Adam Harangozó","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Snail_dish_in_Marrakesh_1.jpg"}},"ingredient/rice-hulls":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/rice-hulls","n":"Rice hulls","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Rice hulls or husks are the hard protecting coverings of grains of rice. In addition to protecting rice during the growing season, rice hulls can be put to use as building material, fertilizer, insulation material, or fuel. Rice hulls are part of the chaff of the rice."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-rice-hulls"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rice_hulls"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine",[["Century egg","dish/century-egg"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Calcium oxide","ingredient/calcium-oxide"],["Clay","ingredient/clay"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Wood ash","ingredient/wood-ash"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/d/d6/Rice_chaffs.jpg/500px-Rice_chaffs.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"No machine-readable author provided. Green assumed (based on copyright claims).","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rice_chaffs.jpg"}},"ingredient/sport-peppers":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/sport-peppers","n":"Sport peppers","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Capsicum annuum is a flowering plant in the family Solanaceae (nightshades), native to the northern regions of South America and to southwestern North America. The plant produces berries of many colors including red, green, and yellow, often with pungent taste. It is one of the oldest cultivated crops, with domestication dating back to around 6,000 years ago in regions of Mexico. The genus Capsicum has over 30 species but Capsicum annuum is the primary species in its genus, as it has been widely cultivated for human consumption for a substantial amount of time and has spread across the world. This species has many uses in culinary and ornamental applications."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-sport-peppers"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sport_peppers"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["American cuisine","cuisine/american-cuisine",[["Chicago-style hot dog","dish/chicago-style-hot-dog"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Celery salt","ingredient/celery-salt"],["Chicago-style relish","ingredient/chicago-style-relish"],["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Hot dog","ingredient/hot-dog"],["Kosher dill pickle","ingredient/kosher-dill-pickle"],["Mustard","ingredient/mustard"],["Mustard (condiment)","ingredient/mustard-condiment"],["Pickled cucumber","ingredient/pickled-cucumber"],["Poppy seed","ingredient/poppy-seed"],["Tomato","ingredient/tomato"],["Vienna Beef","ingredient/vienna-beef"],["Vienna sausage","ingredient/vienna-sausage"],["White onion","ingredient/white-onion"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/d/d2/Capsicum_annuum_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-027.jpg/500px-Capsicum_annuum_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-027.jpg","w":500,"h":619,"by":"Franz Eugen Köhler, Köhler's Medizinal-Pflanzen","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Capsicum_annuum_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-027.jpg"}},"dish/diane-s-place":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/diane-s-place","n":"Diane's Place","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Hmong cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Diane's Place is a restaurant in Minneapolis, Minnesota, United States. It opened in the spring of 2024 in the Food Building in Northeast Minneapolis. The restaurant is owned and operated by chef Diane Moua, who is a James Beard Award-nominated pastry chef. Diane's Place serves breakfast, brunch, lunch, and dinner, featuring dishes that blend Hmong American comfort food with culinary techniques that Moua has learned throughout her career. The menu includes traditional Hmong cuisine using ingredients partly sourced from Moua's parents' farm in Wisconsin."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-diane-s-place"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diane's_Place"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Hmong cuisine","cuisine/hmong-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"ChawJ Creations","ref":"site/chawjcreations-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"chawjcreations.com","note":"Hmong food and storytelling site by Cindy Her, a pastry chef in the Pacific Northwest.","url":"https://chawjcreations.com/tag/hmong/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Hmong cuisine","links":[["Hmong sausage","dish/hmong-sausage"]]}]},"dish/heichinrou-hong-kong":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/heichinrou-hong-kong","n":"Heichinrou Hong Kong","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Cantonese cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Heichinrou Hong Kong is the Hong Kong arm of the Heichinrou brand, separate from Heichinrou (Japan) though historically represented in both markets by Yasuhiro Hayashi (林康弘). The Hong Kong business was established in 1988 and first opened Heichinrou Seafood Restaurant in Tsim Sha Tsui before launching Metropol Restaurant in 1990 at United Centre in Admiralty, a dim sum and banquet focused venue that seated over 100 tables, the largest capacity among its Hong Kong outlets. In subsequent years, Heichinrou Hong Kong expanded with branches in Causeway Bay, Diamond Hill, Kwun Tong, and Central."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-heichinrou-hong-kong"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heichinrou_Hong_Kong"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Cantonese cuisine","cuisine/cantonese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Made With Lau","ref":"site/madewithlau-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"madewithlau.com","note":"Family-run channel and site teaching Cantonese recipes from retired chef Daddy Lau.","url":"https://madewithlau.com","suggested":true,"kind":"youtube"}]},{"h":"More from Cantonese cuisine","links":[["Cantonese restaurant","dish/cantonese-restaurant"],["Char siu","dish/char-siu"],["ClubONE Riviera","dish/clubone-riviera"],["Fook Lam Moon","dish/fook-lam-moon"],["Har gow","dish/har-gow"],["Hong dou tang","dish/hong-dou-tang"],["Jumbo Kingdom","dish/jumbo-kingdom"],["Lap cheong","dish/lap-cheong"],["Lung King Heen","dish/lung-king-heen"],["Mak's Noodle","dish/mak-s-noodle"],["Rice noodle roll","dish/rice-noodle-roll"],["Siu mei","dish/siu-mei"],["Sweet and sour pork","dish/sweet-and-sour-pork"],["Turnip cake","dish/turnip-cake"],["Wong Kei","dish/wong-kei"]]}]},"dish/la-tante-claire":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/la-tante-claire","n":"La Tante Claire","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 2 cuisines","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["La Tante Claire (The Aunt Claire) was a restaurant in Chelsea, London, which opened in 1977. Owned and operated by Pierre Koffmann, it gained three Michelin stars in 1983, and held all three until the restaurant moved premises in 1998."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-la-tante-claire"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/La_Tante_Claire"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["French cuisine","cuisine/french-cuisine"]